    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 462,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 375,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64382,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 298,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Mesonephric remnants and hyperplasia are typically localized in the lateral cervical wall.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:02:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64383,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 298,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Mesonephric remnants and hyperplasia are usually an incidental finding. Hyperplasia may rarely present with abnormal cervical cytology or as a mass or expansion of the cervical wall.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:02:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64384,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 298,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Mesonephric remnants and hyperplasia are detected in as many as 20% of cervixes.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:02:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64385,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 298,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:02:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64386,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 298,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Mesonephric remnants and hyperplasia are thought to arise from residual cells of the mesonephric duct.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:02:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64387,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 298,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Mesonephric remnants and hyperplasia are usually a microscopic finding, but they may form a mass.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:02:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64388,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 298,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Remnants are composed of clusters or linear arrays of small to medium-sized tubules lined by cuboidal cells with scant eosinophilic cytoplasm and round to ovoid bland nuclei, with dense eosinophilic PAS-positive intraluminal secretions. Mitoses are generally absent. Hyperplasia is a proliferation of mesonephric tubules with features similar to those of remnants. It may be of lobular (most common), diffuse, ductal, or mixed type {2252101; 29269124}. Diffuse hyperplasia lacks lobular growth and has tubules that are architecturally simple and separated by stroma. Ductal hyperplasia shows proliferation of ductal structures, which often have clefts; it may be associated with peripheral tubules. Although not necessary for diagnosis, CD10 (luminal/apical), calretinin, GATA3, TTF1, and PAX8 are frequently positive {26135559; 25851711; 22456609}, and ER/PR and p16 are negative {25272301}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:02:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64389,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 298,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Mesonephric glands tend to be located deep within the cervical stroma, where they are not accessible to cervical cytology sampling. However, on rare occasions they extend to the surface of the endocervical canal, where mesonephric hyperplasia can lead to a diagnosis of atypical glandular cells {16181310}. Features that suggest benign mesonephric sampling include loosely cohesive clusters of cuboidal cells with hyperchromatic nuclei but smooth nuclear contours.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:02:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 382,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64390,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 298,
        "headingId": 382,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:02:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 472,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 385,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64391,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 298,
        "headingId": 385,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> small to medium-sized tubules lined by cuboidal cells with scant eosinophilic cytoplasm and round to ovoid bland nuclei.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:02:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
